# YOUR OWN identity and search config. NEVER COMMIT THESE.
#
# These four un-suffixed filenames are what the code actually loads at runtime,
# and between them they hold your legal name, email, phone, street-level
# location, complete employment history, salary floor, commuting radius, and
# the list of employers you are targeting. That is the whole of the personal
# data this tool touches, and committing any of it to a public fork publishes
# a resume plus a job-search plan under your own name.
#
# The tracked *.example.* files beside each one carry an identical schema
# filled in with a fictional persona. Copy the example to the real name and
# edit it in place:
#
#     cp master_resume.example.yaml master_resume.yaml
#     cp profile.example.yaml       profile.yaml
#     cp ideal-jd.example.md        ideal-jd.md
#     cp companies.example.yaml     companies.yaml
#
# Keeping the real files ignored is what makes `git add -A` safe here. If you
# genuinely want to version your own copies, do it in a private repo, never by
# deleting these four lines.
master_resume.yaml
profile.yaml
ideal-jd.md
companies.yaml

# OS / tooling
.DS_Store
.omc/

# Secrets (never commit)
# `*.env` already covers most of these, but each one is named explicitly so a
# repo audit can verify the baseline rather than infer it from a glob.
.env
*.env
.env.*
.mcp-credentials.env
# ...but the committed TEMPLATE must survive those patterns. `.env.*` above
# matches `.env.example`, so without this negation the one env file that is
# meant to be public would be silently untrackable.
!.env.example
secure/
keypairs/

# LinkedIn data export (contains PII; never commit)
linkedin-data/
jobs.db.bak*

# Scratch: interview prep notes and SDD workspaces. Local only, never the
# repo. Swept in by an over-broad `git add -A` in 143dbde; untracked here.
.superpowers/

# open-jobs corpus cache. openjobs.cache_dir() sits beside jobs.db on the host,
# but falls back to the checkout when JOB_DB is unset, and one build of group
# files is ~37MB.
openjobs-cache/
